## 1. Introduction
Pharmaceutically active compounds (PACs) are anthropogenic pollutants that are continuously released into the environment. PACs have been reported to have harmful effects on ecosystems and human health [1]. As a widely used PAC, ibuprofen (IBP) is prevalent in the treatment of pain, fever, acne, and inflammation [2]. With its vast global production and use, IBP has been frequently detected in wastewater, surface water, seawater, and even groundwater, ranging from $\mathrm{\ng/L}$ to $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{L}$ concentrations [3–6]. Therefore, various removal methods have been used to treat IBP in aquatic environments, such as adsorption, biodegradation, chemical oxidation, and nanofiltration [7–11].
Among the existing approaches, advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) are considered excellent for the treatment of organic pollutants [12–13]. Owing to the long lifetime, high oxidation potential, and wide applicable pH range of the sulfate radical $(\mathsf{S}0_{4}^{-\bullet})$ , $S0_{4}^{-8}.$ based AOPs (SR-AOPs) are promising for eliminating organic contaminants [14]. $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ can be produced by the activation of persulfate (PS) or peroxymonosulfate (PMS) by light, ultrasonication, heat, transition metal ions, heterogeneous catalysts, and photocatalytic activation [15–17]. Photocatalytic degradation is an effective environmentally friendly AOP for decomposing organic pollutants [18– 19]. However, SR-AOPs and photocatalysis have their own shortcomings, such as the high recombination rate of photogenerated electron-hole pairs [20]. To overcome this, photocatalysis and SRAOPs are used together to degrade contaminants from water [21,22]. For example, Wang et al. reported that the degradation rate of levofloxacin was $98.1\%$ under a PMS $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\prime}\mathsf{M n O@M n O_{x}}$ system with visible light irradiation [23]. Therefore, it is highly desirable to develop appropriate catalysts that can activate PS for use in photocatalysis.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a hybrid of inorganic and organic porous materials, which have advantages of multiple active sites and specific structural and textural characteristics [24–27]. Fe-based MOFs not only show the preferred photocatalytic activity, but also display great potential for heterogeneous activation of PS [28,29]. For example, MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-88A (Fe) can activate PS to effectively remove tetracycline hydrochloride upon irradiation with visible light [29,30]. Yin et al. [31] achieved $98.9\%$ removal of sulfamethoxazole in a solar/PS/MIL101(Fe) system. Different facets of materials have different surface configurations that can lead to a significant variation in the photocatalytic activity. This is because the facet is closely related to the optical properties, surface electronic structure, and electron migration characteristics of catalysts [32–34]. Recently, many investigations have demonstrated that the nature of the exposed crystal facets plays a significant role in the removal of organic pollutants. Yang et al. [35] reported that the {02 1} facet of the iron-based mineral goethite was more favorable for activating PS than the {110} facet, and goethite with more {021} facets exhibited better catalytic performance for tetracycline degradation. Li et al. [36] found that $\mathsf{F e W O}_{4}$ with {001} facets showed higher redox activity for organic pollutants and Cr (VI) catalytic degradation than $\mathsf{F e W O}_{4}$ with {0 1 0} facets. Similarly, the exposed crystal facets of MOFs determine their catalytic activity.
Herein, three different morphologies of MIL-88B(Fe) were prepared to study the effect of different facets on the photocatalytic degradation of IBP in the presence of PS. MIL-88B(Fe) is mainly composed of {101} and {002} facets, and the {101} facet contains relatively more active sites. Therefore, the number of highly reactive {1 0 1} facets plays an important role in the catalytic activity of MIL-88B(Fe). Furthermore, the presence of the {10 0} facet significantly affected the degradation efficiency of IBP. The catalytic degradation mechanism was explored using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), electrochemical analysis, and density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Radical scavenger and electron spin resonance (ESR) experiments were performed to verify the main active species in IBP degradation. Finally, the possible degradation pathways of IBP catalytic degradation were also proposed.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
All chemicals in this study were used without further purification, and ultrapure water was used to prepare stock solutions for all experiments. The reagents and chemicals are listed in detail in Text S1.
## 2.2. Synthesis and characterization of MIL-88B(Fe)
Three morphologies of MIL-88B(Fe) were prepared using a hydrothermal method based on previous studies [37–39].
MIL-88B(Fe)-1 (M88B1): Terephthalic acid $\left(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C},1.66\ \mathsf{g}\right)$ was dispersed in N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF; $50~\mathrm{mL}$ . $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ $\mathrm{(4.7~mL)}$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(2.7~\mathrm{g})$ , and NaOH $(0.32\ {\ g})$ were added to the mixture with continuous magnetic stirring to form a homogeneous solution. The solution was sealed in a Teflon-lined stainlesssteel autoclave ( $\mathrm{100~mL}$ and placed in an oven at $110^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After separation by centrifugation and washing with $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H}$ several times, the yellow precipitate was separated and dried in an oven for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$
MIL-88B(Fe)-2 (M88B2): $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $(2.7~\mathrm{g})$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ $(1.66~\mathrm{g})$ were dispersed in DMF $50~\mathrm{mL}$ . NaOH (4 mL, $2.0\mathrm{mol/L})$ was added with continuous magnetic stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The obtained solution was sealed in a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and placed in an oven at $100^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After separation by centrifugation and washing with ultrapure water and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{5}{\mathsf{O H}}$ three times, the yellow precipitate was collected and fully dried in an oven for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $75~^{\circ}C$
MIL-88B(Fe)-3 (M88B3): $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $_{(0.622\mathrm{~g})}$ and ${\bf H}_{2}{\tt B D C}$ $(0.382~\mathrm{g})$ were dispersed in DMF $50~\mathrm{mL}$ ) with magnetic stirring to form a homogeneous solution. The obtained solution was sealed in a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and placed in an oven at $145~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After separation by centrifugation and washing with ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{5}{\mathsf{O H}}$ three times, the yellow precipitate was collected and fully dried in an oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $70^{\circ}C$ .
The characterization methods are provided in Text S2.
## 2.3. Experimental procedure and analytical methods
The performance of the as-prepared MIL-88B(Fe) for photocatalysis and PS activation was evaluated in terms of IBP degradation. A photoreactor with a Xe lamp (500 W) and a $420~\mathrm{{nm}}$ cutoff filter was used to conduct the degradation experiments. Typically, $5\mathrm{mg}$ of the catalyst was mixed with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of an aqueous solution of IBP $(50~\mathrm{mg/L})$ with stirring for $120\ \mathrm{min}$ in the dark to reach adsorption–desorption equilibrium. A given quantity of ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}_{2}{\sf O}_{8}$ was added to this solution, and the Xe lamp was turned on. During the reaction, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ samples were withdrawn at given time intervals and filtered with a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ filter for HPLC analysis. All degradation experiments were performed at least in triplicated. The theoretical DFT calculations and the analytical methods related to the detection of IBP are described in Text S3.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the three MIL-88B(Fe) samples are shown in Fig. 1a. All of the XRD characteristic peaks are in good agreement with previous reports [37–39]. Interestingly, the peaks of the M88B1 and M88B3 diffraction patterns are almost the same, apart from the {100} facet that exists in M88B1. Another difference between these two XRD patterns is the intensity of the peaks. However, the diffraction peaks for the characteristic {101} and {002} crystal planes of M88B2 are shifted toward higher and lower diffraction angles, respectively. The reason for this phenomenon may be that the washing solvent of M88B2 is different from that of M88B1 and M88B3, and the washing solvent may induce swelling of the MIL-88B(Fe) frameworks that may shift the peak positions [38,40].
Fig. 1b shows a comparison of the fourier transform-infrared (FTIR) spectra of three MIL-88B(Fe) samples. M88B1, M88B2, and M88B3 share similar FTIR vibrations, demonstrating that they have the same basic structural framework. The characteristic peak at $554~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was assigned to the stretching vibration of the Fe-O group in the iron-oxo clusters. The $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bending vibrations in the benzene rings of the organic linker appeared at $750\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . This suggests that there were iron-oxo clusters present between the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ of ${\mathrm{FeCl}}_{3}$ and the dicarboxylate of the $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ linker [41]. The peaks at $1383~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belong to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ symmetric, asymmetric, and stretching vibrations. The strong bands at $1602~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ and $1663~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned asymmetric stretching vibration of carboxylate linkers and some uncoordinated carboxylic acid groups.
[42,43]. The results of the FTIR spectra further support the successful synthesis of MIL-88B(Fe), which all contained -Fe-O-Felinkages.
As shown in Fig. 2, SEM was used to observe the surface morphology and facet features of the as-prepared samples. Specific facets and morphologies of MIL-88B(Fe) could be obtained by using different reaction times, reaction temperatures, and the addition of different auxiliaries in the reaction (Fig. 2a). Fig. S1 presents the identified and calculated {100}, {101}, and {002} crystallographic facets. The truncated hexagonal cone at the end of M88B1, as displayed in Fig. 2a, can be ascribed to the {10 0} facets. The tip faces of MIL-88B(Fe) are interpreted as {101} facets, and the elongated faces in the middle that are connected to {101} facets are determined to be {00 2} facets (Fig. 2a). From the SEM images, it is revealed that M88B1 has a typical hexagonal prism structure that possesses a truncated hexagonal cone at each end, with a particle size of approximately $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 2b). M88B2 is composed of a well-crystallized smooth rod-like surface morphology with an average particle length of $1-3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. 2c). M88B3 displays a spindle-like structure with a size of approximately $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 2d). The morphologies of all three MIL-88B(Fe) were consistent with those reported in the literature [37–39]. Furthermore, a high degree of uniformity was observed in the SEM images of all three MOF crystals. From the SEM images and the reconstructed geometric model, it is obvious that the {1 0 0} facets only exist in M88B1. Moreover, there is a greater proportion of {101} facets in M88B1 and M88B3 than in M88B2, which is reflected in the catalytic activities of these three MOFs.
$\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms and pore size distributions (Fig. 3a) were used to investigate the specific surface area and porous structures of the three MIL-88B(Fe) samples. The ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms showed type IV adsorption curves, suggesting that all of the samples were mesoporous materials [44–46]. Table S1 summarizes the characteristics of the physical structures [37–39]. The M88B3 structure revealed the typical coexistence of micropores and mesopores, and its adsorption capacity was higher than that of M88B1 and M88B2 [47].
XPS was performed to investigate the chemical composition of different species on the surface of MIL-88B(Fe). Signals for C, O, and Fe were present in the XPS spectra of M88B1, M88B2, and M88B3, which were all similar (Fig. S2-4a). Peaks observed at 284.8 and $288.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ in C 1 s XPS spectra (Fig. S2-4b) are attributed to the $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bonds, respectively, in the benzene ring of the organic ligands. The XPS spectra of the O 1 s region (Fig. S2-4c) contain three diffraction peaks at 530.2, 531.7, and $532.5\ \mathrm{~eV}$ ， assigned to the Fe-O, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , and $0{\mathrm{-H}}$ bonds of MIL-88(Fe), respectively. Fig. 4a shows Fe 2p XPS spectra, where the peaks at 711.7 and $725.2\ \mathrm{~eV}$ are attributed to $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ -based Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, while the peaks at 713.6 and $729.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ -based Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively. In addition, there is a satellite peak at $718.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ that belongs to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . Because PS can be activated by $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ to generate $S0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ (Eq. (1)) [48], the ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ in the three MIL-88B(Fe) samples were compared (Fig. 4b). The ratio of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ decreases in the order of $\mathsf{M88B1}>\mathsf{M88B3}>\mathsf{M88B2}$ , indicating that more $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ exists on the surface of M88B1 than on M88B3 or M88B2. These results imply that a high ratio of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ in the M88B1/PS/light system could lead to a better catalytic activity for M88B1.
