## 3.2. Catalytic performance of MIL-88B(Fe)
The catalytic activity of MIL-88B(Fe) was determined by the degradation of IBP under visible-light irradiation. Control experiments were performed to determine the importance of light and PS on the degradation of IBP in the presence of the catalysts (Fig. S5a–c). Taking M88B1 as an example, the degradation extent of IBP was $73\%$ with PS in the dark whereas it was $84\%$ without PS under visible light for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . This suggests that the illumination of visible light is more important than the presence of PS for this reaction. With both PS and light present, $94\%$ IBP degradation was achieved. These results indicate that the high degradation of IBP could be attributed to the synergetic effect of MIL-88B(Fe), PS, and visible light irradiation. The activities of the three MIL-88B (Fe) were also compared, and the results are shown in Fig. 5a. It was found that M88B1 $(94\%)$ outperformed M88B2 $(48\%)$ and M88B3 $(89\%)$ at a reaction time of $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , indicating the prominent catalytic activity of M88B1. The IBP degradation efficiency decreased in the order $\mathsf{M88B1}>\mathsf{M88B3}>\mathsf{M8}8\mathsf{B2}$ . The reason for this may be due to the {100} facets, which may contain a large number of active sites, and the {10 1} facets, which have more highly active sites than {002} facets. In addition, these results are consistent with the XPS results. Total organic carbon (TOC) was analyzed to determine the mineralization of IBP over M88B1 in the presence of PS (Fig. S5d). Approximately $73\%$ of TOC removal was achieved under $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ of visible light irradiation, suggesting that most of the organic carbon of IBP was removed and converted to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ during the synergetic effect of photodegradation and $S0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ oxidation process.
The impact of influencing variables on the IBP degradation was further investigated. First, the effect of the PS concentration on IBP degradation was studied. As shown in Fig. 5b, as the PS dosage increased, the degradation percentage of IBP distinctly increased to a maximum at $60~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , with further increases in PS concentration resulting in a decline in IBP degradation. This result can be explained as follows. As the yield of photoexcited electron-hole pairs is invariable for a given amount of catalyst, the increasing concentration of PS could react with the electron-hole pairs to generate $S0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ until a stable plateau was reached during the photocatalysis process (Eqs. (1) and (2)). On the other hand, above $60~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , the high concentration of PS resulted in the formation of $S_{2}0_{8}^{-\bullet}$ , which is less reactive than $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ (Eq. (3)) [49]. In addition, excessive $S0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ can be consumed by self-scavenging (Eq. (4)). Thus, the optimal dosage of PS used in this study was $60~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ .
