Obtaining the optimal amount of photocatalyst is of great significance to the removal of IBP. The results of catalyst dosage on IBP degradation are shown in Fig. 5(c). With increased mass of M88B1 added $(<5~\mathrm{mg})$ , the photocatalytic degradation efficiency of IBP gradually increased. However, when the mass of M88B1 was further increased to $10\mathrm{mg}$ or $20\mathrm{mg}$ the photocatalytic degradation rate increases and becomes slow. In order to understand the degradation ability of ibuprofen in a system with a pH of $\begin{array}{r}{{\cal M}88{\cal B}1/.}\end{array}$ - light/PS. Fig. 5(d) shows that the initial pH degradation effect was the best. When the pH of the solution is higher than 4.3, IBP mainly exists in the form of anions, thus in alkaline solutions, IBP and $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ will repel each other, which reduces the degradation of IBP. When the pH of the solution is lower than 4.3, IBP exists in the form of molecules [50]. The degradation efficiency of M88B1 in an alkaline solution $\mathrm{\boldmath~\pH}>7.5\mathrm{\boldmath~\cdot~}$ was poor, which may be caused by the partial decomposition of M88B1 in the solution. Finding the optimal pH of photocatalyst for the degradation of IBP is of great significance.
Inorganic anions naturally present in aqueous environments can react with radicals and might affect the decomposition efficiency of organic contaminants. In this study, the effects of $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ on IBP degradation were evaluated to further investigate the practical use of the M88B1/PS/light system. As illustrated in Fig. 5e, all the anions tested had inhibitory effects on the degradation of IBP. The reason that $S0_{4}^{2-}$ had a moderate inhibitory effect on IBP degradation is that $S0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ and $S_{2}O_{8}^{-}$ can be quenched by excess sulfate [51]. The IBP degradation efficiency in the M88B1/PS/light system was reduced after the addition of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ , as ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ could react with $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ and $\boldsymbol{\cdot}_{\mathrm{OH}}$ to produce NO3, which is less reactive than $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ (Eqs. (5) and (6)) [52]. Similarly, the presence of ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}$ slightly inhibits IBP degradation, because a reaction could occur between $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ and $S0_{4}^{-\bullet}/{}^{\bullet}\mathrm{OH}$ that would generate the less reactive $\mathbf{\nabla}C^{\bullet}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}^{-\bullet}$ species (Eqs. (7)–(9)) [53].
