## 3.3. Stability of MIL-88B(Fe) composite
To evaluate the feasibility of the catalyst in practical applications, the reusability and cycle stability of MIL-88B(Fe) were also studied. As shown in Fig. S6a, after three cycles of degradation of IBP by the MIL-88B(Fe) photocatalyst, its photocatalytic activity did not significantly decrease, indicating that the MIL-88B(Fe) photocatalyst is highly stable and can be used for repeated treatment of IBP. No significant difference was observed between the FTIR spectra of the test sample and the fresh sample (Fig. S6b), demonstrating the excellent stability and reusability of MIL-88B(Fe) in the photocatalytic process.
## 3.4. Scavenger experiments and ESR analysis
Free radical scavenger experiments and ESR analyses were conducted to identify the contributions of the generated radicals in the M88B1/PS/light system. It has been reported that $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ can be trapped by tert-butanol (TBA), whereas methanol (MeOH) can scavenge both $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ . Benzoquinone BQ can quench both $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ , but it has a stronger reaction with $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ and a weaker reaction with OH [54] and $\mathrm{\DeltaL}$ -histidine was selected as a scavenger to quench ${}^{1}0_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 5f, all three radical scavengers significantly inhibited the decomposition of IBP. In the first $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , the inhibitory ability of the four scavengers was weak, and the inhibitory ability gradually becomes stronger as the reaction progresses. Furthermore, the inhibitory effect of TBA significantly increased as the TBA concentration increased from 100 to $500~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . Therefore, $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ , OH, $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ , and ${}^{1}0_{2}$ were all responsible for the catalytic degradation of IBP in the M88B1/PS/light system. As shown in Fig. 6, the same results were observed by ESR spectroscopy: the signals of DMPO- $.0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ (Fig. 6a), DMPO- $^{\bullet}\mathrm{OH}$ and DMPO- $.S0_{4}^{-8}$ (Fig. 6b), and
TEMP- $^10_{2}$ (Fig. 6c) were detected, indicating that $|0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ , OH, $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ and $^10_{2}$ radicals were produced in the M88B1/PS/light system.
## 3.5. Proposed mechanism
The UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) of the three MIL88B(Fe) were measured to determine the catalytic degradation mechanism (Fig. 7a). The main absorbances of M88B1, M88B2, and M88B3 all appear in the range of $200{-}600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, the absorption intensity order of the MIL-88B(Fe) phases is $\mathrm{M88B1~>~M88B3~>~M88B2}$ , revealing that M88B1 possesses a greater visible light absorption capacity than M88B3 and M88B2. This result is consistent with the IBP catalytic degradation performance of the three MIL-88B(Fe) phases. In addition, using the Tauc plot method, the optical band gaps of M88B1, M88B2, and M88B3 were measured as 2.21, 2.44, and $2.29\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively [55]. Therefore, M88B1 has the smallest band gap and highest light absorption of the three MIL-88B(Fe) phases, giving it more active sites for the photocatalytic degradation of IBP from the same intensity solar light compared to the other two phases. DFT was used to calculate the total density of states (DOS) to explore the potential photocatalytic activity of MIL-88B(Fe) during the IBP degradation process. The band gap calculated for MIL-88B(Fe) was 2.24 eV (Fig. 8), which is only slightly different to that obtained by the Tauc plot method. It can be concluded from the band gap value that MIL88B(Fe) has a good capacity for absorbing visible light to enhance the degradation efficiency of IBP. Furthermore, from the total DOS (Fig. 8) and DOS of Fe, O, and C (Fig. S7), the valence band (VB) is mainly composed of $\mathtt{C2p}$ and O 2p orbitals, while the conduction band (CB) is dominated by Fe 3d orbitals. Therefore, the Fe center might act as an active site for the degradation of IBP, with MIL-88B (Fe) producing electron-hole $({\mathsf{e}}^{-}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{h}}^{+})$ pairs under visible light irradiation (Eq. (10)).
