$$
To further evaluate the photoexcited charge separation and migration efficiency, electrochemical experiments and photoluminescence (PL) measurements were conducted. As shown in Fig. 9a, all three MIL-88B(Fe) catalysts displayed clear photocurrent responses under visible light irradiation. Notably, M88B1 exhibited the strongest photocurrent density among the three catalysts, suggesting that the charge separation and transfer characteristics are the best because M88B1 has the characteristic {10 0} facets. As shown in Fig. 9b, M88B1 has the smallest semicircle depicted in the Nyquist plots, which implies that M88B1 possesses the lowest electron transfer resistance [56]. The PL spectra (Fig. 9c) show that M88B1 has a low PL emission, while M88B2 has a high PL peak intensity, indicating that M88B1 has a faster charge transfer ability with a prolonged lifetime of electron-hole pairs.
$$
\mathrm{SO_{4}^{-}+H_{2}O\ \rightarrow\ S O_{4}^{2-}+\cdot O H\ +\ H^{+}}
$$
$$
{\bf0}_{2}+{\bf M I L-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})(\mathrm{e^{-}})\rightarrow{\bf0}_{2}^{-}+{\bf M I L-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})
$$
$$
20_{2}^{-}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}0\rightarrow10_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}0_{2}+2\mathrm{H}0^{-}
$$
According to these results, the possible catalytic reaction mechanism of IBP in the MIL-88B(Fe)/PS/light system is depicted in Fig. 10. First, electron-hole pairs can be generated from visible light irradiation. On the one hand, the photogenerated electrons can react with $0_{2}$ to generate $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ (Eq. (2) and (12)). On the other hand, $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ on the surface of MIL-88B(Fe) can also activate PS into $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ (Eq. (1)). Meanwhile, $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ on the surface of MIL-88B(Fe) could be transformed into $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ upon irradiation with visible light or reacted with PS to generate $S_{2}0_{8}^{-\bullet}$ (Eq. (14)) [61], which promotes the generation of $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ . In addition, it is worth mentioning that the photogenerated electrons accelerated the activation of PS into $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ through Eq. (2). Subsequently, $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ , $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ , and $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ react with IBP, leading to its degradation (Eq. (15)).
$$
\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}@\mathrm{MIL-88B(Fe)}~+~\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}^{2-}\to~\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}@\mathrm{MIL-88B(Fe)}~+~\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}^{-}
$$
## 3.6. Possible degradation pathways
The organic products of IBP catalytic degradation were determined by time of flight mass spectrometry (TOF-MS), allowing the proposal of the possible degradation pathways of IBP in the M88B1/PS/light system (Fig. 11). Fig. S9 shows the original data of TOF-MS. The organic compound detected at $m/z\ =\ 205$ was IBP. Seven intermediate products were detected in addition to
IBP. Product 1 with $m/z=221$ was the hydroxylate product of IBP generated by the attack of $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ , which further confirmed that $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ existed in the system. With further oxidation of product 1 by $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ , OH, $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ , and $^10_{2}$ , product 4 $(m/z=177)$ and product 5 $(m/z=133)$ 1 were generated. Since $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ , $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ , $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ , and $^10_{2}$ could oxidize IBP, some other intermediates such as products 3 and 6 were produced. Moreover, during the decarboxylation of IBP and product 3, products 2 $(m/z=161)$ and 7 $(m/z=133)$ were formed, respectively. Finally, all these organic products were mineralized into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ via the oxidation of $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ , OH, $0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ , and $^10_{2}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, three different morphologies of MIL-88B(Fe) were used to investigate the impact of facets on the catalytic degradation of IBP in the presence of PS under visible light irradiation. The results suggested that the existence of the {10 0} facet and a higher $\{101\}/\{002\}$ facet proportion resulted in greater IBP catalytic degradation and PS activation. Of the three MIL-88B(Fe) morphologies tested, M88B1 possessed the best catalytic activity for IBP degradation, while M88B2 possessed the worst. DFT calculations were used to estimate the energy of the crystallographic facets of MIL-88B(Fe) and to further confirm the mechanism of MIL-88B(Fe) as a catalyst through DOS and band structure analysis. The presence of $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ hindered the degradation of IBP because they could react with the radicals generated from the reaction process. Quenching experiments and ESR spectra demonstrated the contribution of $S O_{4}^{-\bullet}$ , OH, $|0_{2}^{-\bullet}$ , and $^10_{2}$ in the IBP degradation process. In addition, seven intermediate products were detected, and the possible degradation pathways of IBP were identified.
